      "content": "I'll focus on the logistics and safety protocols.\n\nTemperature thresholds for tuna are critical. According to food safety guidelines, tuna should be stored at a temperature below 2°C (35.6°F) to prevent bacterial growth and spoilage. The ideal storage temperature for tuna is between -1.8°C and 2°C (28.8°F and 35.6°F).\n\nIf the temperature in the container rises to 12°C (53.6°F) and stays there for more than an hour, it's likely that the tuna has been compromised. Bacteria like Staphylococcus aureus and Clostridium botulinum can grow rapidly between 5°C and 15°C (41°F and 59°F), and at 12°C, the risk of spoilage increases significantly.\n\nTo determine the fate of the tuna, you'll need to assess the duration of the temperature spike and the overall condition of the product. Here are some general guidelines:\n\n* If the temperature spike was brief (less than 30 minutes), the tuna might still be safe if it's stored at a lower temperature immediately after the spike.\n* If the temperature spike lasted between 30 minutes to 2 hours, the tuna may still be",
